.TH "structs" 3 "Version 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
structs \- any CBOR into a C++ struct and back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SS "Classes"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "struct \fBcbor::member< P >\fP"
.br
.RI "One entry of \fBcbor::describe_members\fP: the key of a member and a pointer to that member\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "struct \fBcbor::describe_members< T >\fP"
.br
.RI "Describes how \fBcbor::structs\fP and cbor::schema write and read \fRT\fP\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "class \fBcbor::structs< T >\fP"
.br
.RI "Writes a C++ value as CBOR and reads it back\&. "
.in -1c
.SS "Macros"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "#define \fBCBOR_MEMBER\fP(T,  m)"
.br
.RI "Makes the \fBcbor::member\fP entry of \fRT::m\fP with the text of the name \fRm\fP as its key\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "#define \fBCBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS\fP(T,  TAG, \&.\&.\&.)"
.br
.RI "Writes the \fBcbor::describe_members\fP specialization of \fRT\fP with the tag \fRTAG\fP and one CBOR_MEMBER entry for each name in the list\&. "
.in -1c
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
\fR\fBcbor::structs\fP<T>\fP writes a C++ value as one CBOR item\&. It reads one CBOR item back into a value of type \fRT\fP\&. Include \fR<\fBcbor/cbor\&.hpp\fP>\fP\&.

.PP
A struct is written as a map\&. Each public data member is one entry, and its key is the name of the member\&. A description changes the keys, adds a tag, or names the members of a class\&. See KEYS AND DESCRIPTIONS\&.

.PP
\fR\fBcbor::structs\fP\fP finds the members of an aggregate by reflection\&. On g++ 16 with \fR-std=c++26 -freflection\fP, the members come from reflection\&. On every other compiler, Boost\&.PFR 1\&.84 or later finds them\&. If the system has no Boost\&.PFR, the build uses the copy that the library ships\&. A class with a base class, and a class that is not an aggregate, needs a description\&. Every translation unit of a program must see the same Boost\&.PFR\&. A program that also uses another Boost\&.PFR of its own breaks the one definition rule\&. An older Boost\&.PFR that comes first on the include path stops the build with an error that names version 1\&.84\&. An install places the shipped copy in \fRinclude/mbedded-cbor/boost_pfr\fP\&. The CMake package and the pkg-config file add it to the include path\&.
.SH "WRITE A VALUE"
.PP
\fR\fBcbor::structs\fP<T>::encode(value)\fP gives \fRstd::expected<std::string, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP, the bytes of the item\&.

.PP
\fR\fBcbor::structs\fP<T>::encode(value, target)\fP appends the bytes to \fRtarget\fP\&. It gives \fRstd::expected<std::size_t, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP, the count of bytes that it appended\&. The target is a \fRstd::string\fP, a container of \fRchar\fP, a \fRstd::span<char>\fP, or a writer of the program\&. A \fRstd::span<char>\fP that is too small for the bytes gives \fRno_buffer_space\fP\&.

.PP
The keys of a struct come in the order of RFC 8949 section 4\&.2\&.1\&. The functions write no shared value (tag 28 and tag 29)\&.

.PP
The encoder writes a float or a double in the shortest width that holds its value exactly (RFC 8949 section 4\&.1)\&. A NaN keeps its bits:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
A double NaN takes the binary16 width when the low 42 bits of its significand are zero\&. It takes the binary32 width when the low 29 bits are zero\&. Else it takes the binary64 width\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A float NaN takes the binary16 width when the low 13 bits of its significand are zero\&. Else it takes the binary32 width\&.
.PP
.SH "READ A VALUE"
.PP
\fR\fBcbor::structs\fP<T>::decode\fP reads one CBOR item\&. It does not read a byte after the item\&. It gives \fRstd::expected<\fBcbor::ref\fP<T>, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP\&. The ref holds the owner of the bytes\&. \fR*result\fP is the ref, and \fR**result\fP is the value of \fRT\fP\&. A \fRstd::string_view\fP or a \fRstd::span\fP in \fRT\fP points into the bytes\&. It stays valid while the ref lives\&. A result with no view member holds its own copy of every value\&. No form copies the bytes\&.

.PP
The forms are:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdecode(std::move(encoded))\fP: \fRencoded\fP is an rvalue of \fRstd::string\fP or of another owning range of \fRchar\fP\&. The bytes move into a new owner\&. An lvalue does not compile\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdecode(owner)\fP: \fRowner\fP is a \fRstd::shared_ptr\fP to a range of \fRchar\fP\&. The result keeps the owner\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdecode(owner, target)\fP: the read puts the values into the containers of \fRtarget\fP, and it uses their allocator\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdecode(owner, part)\fP: \fRpart\fP is a \fRstd::string_view\fP into memory that \fRowner\fP, a \fRstd::shared_ptr<void const>\fP, keeps alive\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdecode(owner, part, target)\fP: the same, and the read puts the values into the containers of \fRtarget\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
A \fRstd::string\fP lvalue, a \fRstd::string_view\fP alone and a \fRchar const *\fP do not compile\&.

.PP
The forms without \fRtarget\fP start from \fRT{}\fP, so \fRT\fP must be default constructible\&. A null or empty owner throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP\&.

.PP
A \fRtarget\fP of \fRstd::pmr\fP containers sets the memory resource of each element\&. When the resource cannot give memory, the read gives \fRnot_enough_memory\fP\&. The decoder reserves no memory for a count that the input states\&. It makes each element when it reads that element\&.
.SH "TYPES"
.PP
\fRT\fP, and each member of a struct in \fRT\fP, has one of these C++ types\&. The CBOR item is on the right:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbool\fP: \fRtrue\fP or \fRfalse\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::nullptr_t\fP: \fRnull\fP\&. \fRcbor::simple_value\fP: a simple value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
An integer type of 8 to 64 bits: an integer\&. Where the compiler has \fR__int128\fP, that type and its unsigned form give an integer or a bignum (tag 2 or 3)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
An enum with a fixed underlying type: the integer of that type\&. \fRdecode\fP does not compile for an enum with no fixed underlying type, because some values of its underlying type are not values of the enum\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRfloat\fP and \fRdouble\fP: the shortest float that holds the value exactly\&. \fRdecode\fP takes a binary16, binary32 or binary64 item\&. A binary64 item that a \fRfloat\fP cannot hold exactly gives \fRnumber_out_of_range\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::string\fP, \fRstd::pmr::string\fP and \fRstd::string_view\fP: a text string\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::span<std::byte const>\fP: a byte string\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::vector\fP or \fRstd::array\fP of \fRstd::byte\fP, of an integer of 8 to 64 bits, of \fRfloat\fP or of \fRdouble\fP: a typed array of RFC 8746, in little endian\&. The tag is the one of the element type\&. For example, tag 64 is for \fRstd::byte\fP and \fRstd::uint8_t\fP\&. \fRdecode\fP takes this item only\&. A plain array gives \fRincorrect_type\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::set\fP and \fRstd::flat_set\fP: tag 258 on an array, as the CBOR sets specification defines it\&. \fRdecode\fP takes the items in any order\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::chrono::sys_seconds\fP: tag 1 on an integer of seconds since 1970-01-01\&. \fRdecode\fP also takes a float whose value is a whole number\&. \fRdecode\fP gives a NaN back with the same bits\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::chrono::year_month_day\fP: tag 100 on an integer of days since 1970-01-01 (RFC 8943)\&. \fRencode\fP throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP for a date that is not valid\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Where the standard library has \fRstd::chrono::parse\fP, \fRdecode\fP also takes the date-time string of tag 0 for a \fRsys_time\fP, and the full-date string of tag 1004 for a \fRyear_month_day\fP\&. The letters \fRT\fP and \fRZ\fP may be lower case\&. A fraction of a second needs a \fRsys_time\fP that holds it, for example \fRstd::chrono::sys_time<std::chrono::milliseconds>\fP\&. A \fRsys_seconds\fP gives \fRincorrect_type\fP for a fraction, and every type gives \fRincorrect_type\fP for second 60\&. Where the standard library has no \fRstd::chrono::parse\fP (libc++), tag 0 and tag 1004 give \fRincorrect_type\fP\&. A program that needs them brings a type of its own\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::optional<E>\fP: the item of \fRE\fP, or \fRnull\fP when the optional is empty\&. An empty optional member has no key in the map\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::variant<E\&.\&.\&.>\fP: the item of the alternative that the variant holds\&. \fRdecode\fP gives the first alternative that accepts the item\&. An integer alternative accepts an integer that it can hold\&. With \fRstd::variant<std::uint8_t, std::int64_t, std::string>\fP, the item 256 gives the \fRstd::int64_t\fP alternative\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::tuple<E\&.\&.\&.>\fP and \fRstd::array<E, N>\fP of other types: an array of exactly that count\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::map\fP, \fRstd::unordered_map\fP and their \fRstd::pmr\fP forms: a map\&. \fRstd::multimap\fP and \fRstd::unordered_multimap\fP: a map from each key to the array of its values\&. RFC 8949 section 5\&.6 makes a map with a repeated key invalid\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::vector\fP, \fRstd::deque\fP, \fRstd::list\fP and other containers: an array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcbor::tagged_item<N, E>\fP: tag N on the item of \fRE\fP\&. \fRE\fP is not a struct\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A struct: a map\&. See KEYS AND DESCRIPTIONS\&.
.PP
.SH "KEYS AND DESCRIPTIONS"
.PP
The key of a member is its name, as a text string\&. A description sets another key, or a tag\&. Each macro writes its description at global scope, after the type and after the description of each struct that the type holds\&. A description that no call uses is not checked\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fBCBOR_MEMBER(T, m)\fP\fP: the entry of the member \fRm\fP of \fRT\fP\&. The key is the text of the name \fRm\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fBCBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(T, tag, m\&.\&.\&.)\fP\fP: the description of \fRT\fP from the names of its members\&. Each key is the name of its member\&. The list takes up to 343 names\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRCBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(T, tag, entries\&.\&.\&.)\fP: the description of \fRT\fP from entries\&. Each entry is \fR\fBcbor::member\fP{key, &T::m}\fP\&. A key is a text string, or an integer from -2^63 to 2^64 - 1\&. No C++ literal holds -2^63\&. Use \fRstd::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::min()\fP for that key\&.
.PP

.PP
Each macro writes a specialization of \fR\fBcbor::describe_members\fP<T>\fP\&. It has a \fRtag\fP member of type \fRstd::optional<\fBcbor::tag\fP>\fP and a \fRmembers\fP member that is a \fRstd::tuple\fP of \fR\fBcbor::member\fP\fP entries\&. \fRcbor::schema<T>\fP reads the same description\&.

.PP
The tag is \fR\fBcbor::tag\fP{n}\fP, or \fRcbor::untagged\fP for a map with no tag\&. A struct whose description has a tag carries that tag\&. \fRdecode\fP gives \fRincorrect_type\fP when the tag is missing or is another tag\&. An untagged struct gives \fRincorrect_type\fP for a tag in front of its map\&.

.PP
The rules for a description are these:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Each entry names a public, non-static data member of the type, or of a public base class\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
With reflection, the entries are exactly the public data members of the type and of its public bases\&. Another list does not compile\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A member that comes twice, two members with one key, a key of \fRbool\fP or of a character type, a tag of a standard type (for example 1, 258 or a tag of RFC 8746), and an invalid tag do not compile\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
An annotation \fR[[=cbor::key("x")]]\fP or \fR[[=\fBcbor::tag(n)\fP]]\fP on a type that \fR\fBcbor::structs\fP\fP reads does not compile\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A class with a base class uses \fRCBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE\fP or \fRCBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE_MEMBERS\fP\&. These take the base class after the type\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A type whose name holds a comma needs a type alias for the macros\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A class with no default constructor cannot use the forms without \fRtarget\fP\&. It decodes into the target that the caller gives: \fRdecode(owner, target)\fP\&.
.PP
.SH "INPUT FROM OTHER PROGRAMS"
.PP
\fRdecode\fP reads the entries of a map in any order\&. It skips an entry whose key no member has, whatever the type of its key or of its value\&. An array, a map or a tag is such a key\&. So a program reads the bytes of a newer program that added members\&. A new member of type \fRstd::optional\fP also reads the bytes of an older program\&.
.SH "SHARED VALUES"
.PP
\fRdecode\fP reads tag 28 and tag 29 of value sharing (cbor\&.schmorp\&.de)\&. It uses a table that lasts for one call\&. \fRencode\fP writes no shared value\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
A tag 28 is allowed on each member\&. It keeps the value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
No member takes a tag 29\&. A tag 29 gives an error: \fRsharedref_index_not_marked\fP when no tag 28 comes before the index of the tag 29; \fRsharedref_not_complete\fP when that tag 28 is in a member that \fRdecode\fP skips; \fRincorrect_type\fP for any other tag 29\&.
.PP
.SH "ERRORS"
.PP
Each function gives a \fR\fBcbor::error\fP\fP in its \fRstd::expected\fP:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRincorrect_type\fP: an item of another major type; a missing or another tag on a struct with a tag; a tag 29; a variant with no alternative that accepts the item; a plain array for a \fRstd::vector\fP or \fRstd::array\fP of a number type; an array of another count for a \fRstd::tuple\fP or a \fRstd::array\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnumber_out_of_range\fP: an integer that the C++ type cannot hold; a binary64 that a \fRfloat\fP cannot hold exactly; a float under tag 1 whose value is not a whole number\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRinadmissible_type_for_tag_content\fP: a tag 1 over an item that is not an integer or a float\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRkey_not_found\fP: a member that is not \fRstd::optional\fP has no key in the map\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRduplicate_key\fP: a key of a member, or a key of a set or a map, that comes twice\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtoo_little_data\fP: the input ends inside the item\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsyntax_error\fP: a head is not well formed\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRindefinite_length\fP: a head has indefinite length\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstring_length_exceeded\fP, \fRcontainer_elements_exceeded\fP, \fRnesting_depth_exceeded\fP: a string or a container is longer than a limit of the library, or the item nests too deep\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsharedref_index_not_marked\fP, \fRsharedref_not_complete\fP: see SHARED VALUES\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnot_enough_memory\fP: a refused allocation of \fRdecode\fP, or a refusal of a \fRstd::pmr\fP resource\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRno_buffer_space\fP: a \fRstd::span<char>\fP target that is too small\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRvalue_too_large\fP: the size of the bytes does not fit in \fRstd::size_t\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
These functions throw instead:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::logic_error\fP: \fRdecode\fP with a null or empty owner; \fRencode\fP of a \fRstd::chrono::year_month_day\fP that is not a valid date\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::bad_alloc\fP: \fRencode\fP when the allocation of the result is refused\&.
.PP

.PP
 
.SH "EXAMPLES"
 
.PP
.nf
    // person: struct { std::string name; int age; }
    // encoded: 16 bytes, {"age": 30, "name": "ann"}
    auto const encoded = cbor::structs<person>::encode(person{"ann", 30});
    // back: a ref to person{"ann", 30}
    auto const back = cbor::structs<person>::decode(std::string(*encoded));
    auto const age = (*back)\->age;    // 30
    auto const name = (*back)\->name;  // "ann"
.fi
.PP
 
